":"PIT","distance":0.3536,"shared":4,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bears are 8-2 through week 11 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 262 to 197. The offense ranks 11th in EPA per play and the defense 7th in EPA allowed, so neither side has stood out much yet. What sets them apart is the highest play-action rate in the league.","offense":"The Bears offense has been a little above average by EPA per play so far. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the highest play-action rate in the league. Caleb Williams has run the passing game, with Luther Burden III leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Bears have one of the best defenses in the league this season, ranking 7th or better in three of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area has been sack rate, where it ranks 2nd, and it has no real weak spot. Montez Sweat has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Jeffery Simmons, who has 5 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of defenders in the box that ranks 7th highest in the league.","special":"The Bears have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 81% of field goals, which is a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into tight ends, about 8% of the money on the books.","players":"Caleb Williams has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,727 yards. Targets have been spread around, with Luther Burden III and Colston Loveland leading a group where nobody has more than a fifth of the looks. D'Andre Swift has handled most of the work in the backfield with 58% of the carries. On defense, Austin Booker leads the team with 5 sacks and Dillon Thieneman has picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Ben Johnson is calling the offense as head coach and Dennis Allen is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2025 Vikings under Kevin O'Connell and the 2022 Lions under Ben Johnson. Their defense looks most like the 2024 Jets under Jeff Ulbrich and the 2024 Patriots.","trends":"The offense went from 8th in EPA per play in 2025 to 11th so far this season, a modest decline. The defense moved from 19th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 7th so far this season, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was two point attempt rate, which went from 12% to 0%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}